course: dictionary
lesson: 1586
title: Chattering
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: «The girls were chattering in the corridor.» Chattering means talking quickly and a lot about small things. The clues are girls and corridor: people talking in a busy hallway, not a bird or a machine. Ask: what word would fit here? Talking fits, so chattering means talking.
explain: The same word can describe other things. «My teeth were chattering.» Teeth cannot talk. Here chattering means knocking together quickly. The clue is the cold or scared feeling in the sentence, so the body is shaking.
explain: In stories, chattering can describe animals. Birds, monkeys and squirrels make quick, high, repeated sounds, so a tree full of chattering birds is a noisy scene. The clue is the animal, not a person.
fact: Chatter is the verb, and it changes to chatters, chattered and chattering.
fact: Chattering can also be a noun, meaning the quick, busy sound of talk or knocking. It has no plural.
fact: «Chattering» means talking quickly about small, unimportant things.
fact: «Chattering» can mean knocking together quickly, as teeth do when you are cold or scared. In science lessons this is a sign the body is shivering to make heat.
fact: «Chattering» can also mean making quick rattling noises, as machines do. The chattering classes (UK, informal) means educated people who like to discuss news and politics.
exercise: «The girls were chattering in the corridor.» What does chattering mean here? | Knocking together because they are cold | *Talking quickly and a lot | Sitting very still and quiet | Running up and down the stairs
exercise: «My teeth were chattering.» Why might teeth chatter? | *Because you are cold or scared | Because you have just won a prize | Because you are fast asleep | Because you are eating a toffee
exercise: «The real bald eagle has a weak, high chattering call.» What kind of sound is a chattering call? | *A quick, high, repeated sound | A soft whisper | A deep, slow boom | A loud, rattling crash
test: «Cats sometimes make a fast chattering or clicking sound at birds through a window.» What is the cat doing with its sound? | Sleeping on the window sill | Barking at a dog | Singing a slow song | *Making quick, repeated sounds at the birds
test: A worn-out printer starts chattering. What is it doing? | Talking to the children | Sleeping quietly in the corner | Printing in colour | *Making quick rattling noises
exam: What does «the chattering classes» (UK, informal) mean? | *Educated people who like to discuss news and politics | Children chatting in a noisy classroom | Birds sitting on a wire | People who are shivering with cold
